  "subdivision_of_building": "Perimeter fencing to Courts 7–10 only (external structure – no part of any building affected).",
  "status": "Application Under Consideration",
  "description": "Retrospective planning approval is sought for the installation of linear LED lighting mounted to the existing perimeter fencing of Courts 7–10. These courts were previously unlit. The lighting has been affixed directly to the fence line, with no increase to the height, footprint, or alignment of the existing fencing.\n\nThe luminaires are supported by slim horizontal bars fixed across the top of the fence. These supports do not alter the overall height of the structure, are visually consistent with the existing fencing, and are finished in RAL 6005 green to match. The fittings themselves are low‑profile, linear LED luminaires. At the time of installation, it was understood that this constituted a minor operational improvement rather than development requiring express planning permission.\n\nA full lighting design and obtrusive light assessment was undertaken prior to installation. The report confirms that the scheme operates fully within the limits applicable to an E3 environmental zone, with obtrusive light levels remaining below 10 lux. The maximum calculated vertical illuminance at any residential façade is below 10 lux. \n\nThe lighting has been designed to provide targeted, energy‑efficient illumination to the playing surface only, using directional linear LEDs rather than traditional column‑mounted floodlighting. This approach significantly reduces light spill, glare, and visual impact while improving the safe and effective use of the courts\n\nThe luminaires were initially installed without any downward tilt on 17th December 2025. On 14th January 2026, the Planning Officer, Tabatha Hannam, granted permission for the lighting to be operated for a short period (15 minutes on a weekday before 18:30). This allowed for the adjustment of luminaire tilt to align with the lighting design, ensuring that both on-court performance and obtrusive light levels reflect the assessed results.",
  "decision_conditions": null,
